t","label":"Comment","html_id":"comment","implicit":true,"key":"ThEWPOhdDc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall the pierced ears example. We checked the independence of the events M (being a male) and E (having pierced ears) by comparing P(E) to P(E | M).","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"MxY3G2xVBR"}],"key":"cAG0YXeRiN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An alternative method of checking for dependence would be to compare P(E | M) with P(E | not M) [same as P(E | F)]. In our case, P(E | M) = 36/180 = 0.2, while P(E | not M) = 288/320 = 0.9, and since the two are very different, we can say that the events E and M are not independent.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"bYil9WW2lS"}],"key":"Zi8ZdTel86"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In general, another method for checking the independence of events A and B is to compare ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"jpApeRNUIL"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(B | A)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"x3uTq88iXk"}],"key":"KwIJ7H5GAY"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"kquzhCHddj"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(B | not A)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"MnQMVDJcLo"}],"key":"jDDPusKwRI"},{"type":"text","value":". In other words, two events ar