:[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Total","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"L8qx5Xumu7"}],"key":"BdeBq2aH5J"}],"key":"H93P9DpflB"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"45","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"VEuLoJkGpv"}],"key":"kzoUwbECaa"}],"key":"ICmp832kol"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"55","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"lu1nJZpVWT"}],"key":"tKPJ8kVFuJ"}],"key":"sGkYWswzig"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"100","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"V8gViJSBUC"}],"key":"xqDEDSYcXy"}],"key":"veVBxVJ9M6"}],"key":"r5nwPpNzW7"}],"key":"RLqYRZAjB6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offhand, we wouldn’t necessarily have any compelling reason to expect that deciding on a major would depend on a student’s gender. We can check for independence by comparing the overall probability of being decided to the probability of being decided given that a student is female:","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"kezuqNLymV"}],"key":"aOrXAZhsbm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(D) = 45/100 = 0.45 and P(D | F) = 27/60 = 0.45.","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"E7rIAURrGu"}],"key":"DyB3tkmGA4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fact that the two are equal tells us that, as we might expect, deciding on a major is independent of gender. Note from the comment that these must also equal P(D | M), which is 18/40 = 0.45.","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"nW1PITfY1X"}],"key":"kv3Ytk89SD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now let’s approach the issue of independence in a different way: first, we may note that the overall probability of being decided is 45/100 = 0.45, and the overall probability of being female is 60/100 = 0.60.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"VWASi4j137"}],"key":"YmwOax1AxW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If being decided is independent of gender, then 45% of the 60% of the class who are female should have a decided major; in other words, the probability of being female and decided should equal the probability of being female multiplied by the probability of being decided. If the events F and D are independent, we should have P(F and D) = P(F) × P(D).","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"aSAp6LZw2t"}],"key":"oeDTZCt6G0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In fact, P(F and D) = 27/100 = 0.27 = P(F) × P(D) = 0.60 × 0.45. This confirms our alternate verification of independence.","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"CNwdss8FvS"}],"key":"dGsaWB7gXF"}],"class":"tip","key":"a9yQvK5uUm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In general, another method for checking the independence of events A and B is to compare P(A and B) to P(A) × P(B). If the two are equal, then A and B are independent; otherwise the two are not independent.","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"key":"kYLX4yiw5O"}],"key":"F5mVNEZK0s"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s summarize all the possible methods we’ve seen for checking the independence of events in one rule:","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"lcL9RTd8ee"}],"key":"q4rrxBO0SH"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Checking Independence","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"IeeGc04LTR"}],"key":"qSNbaLunjo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two events A and B are independent if any one of the following holds:","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"w9xCTvRdJ6"}],"key":"N8ochdThLe"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":70,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(B | A) = P(B)","position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":70,"column":1}},"key":"b1jghaF7Nx"}],"key":"VNHsyswNb0"}],"key":"NgB2pPmhJ8"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(A | B) = P(A)","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"gqyAExsOrq"}],"key":"XxtFhMFHmH"}],"key":"VyuiI8oOBe"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(B | A) = P(B | not A)","position":{"start":{"line":72,"column":1},"end":{"line":72,"column":1}},"key":"PHoQGwqGP4"}],"key":"OLUWa4fUZe"}],"key":"JahLaanVZE"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"yabOTbn8o2"}],"key":"Vqn9Zy8mht"}],"key":"HOya0UUWk0"}],"key":"MzKufrJhMy"}],"class":"note","key":"jvvj8Arpfq"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"XhjKPfyQT2"}],"identifier":"comment","label":"Comment","html_id":"comment-1","implicit":true,"key":"o7nD16ReLT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"These various equalities turn out to be equivalent, so that if one equality holds, all are equal, and if one equality does not hold, all are not equal. (This is the case for the same reason that knowing one of the values P(A and B), P(A and not B), P(not A and B), or P(not A and not B), along with P(A) and P(B), allows you to determine the remaining cells of a two-way probability table.)","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"dp8pWUMwCm"}],"key":"hRcdtcba54"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"st